UK Banking Adverse News Weekly | 28 September =E2=80=93 04 October 2026

Verified adverse news concerning UK-registered banks

Executive Summary

During the reporting week of 28 September =E2=80=93 04 October 2026, 8 verifi=
ed adverse incidents were recorded across five UK-registered banking groups (=
Barclays, Close Brothers, HSBC UK, NatWest Group, and Santander UK), organise=
d under the report=E2=80=99s five classification themes.

Principal developments included temporary digital access outages at Santander=
 UK and Barclays UK, physical ATM ram-raid damage at Royal Bank of Scotland, =
and an additional =C2=A3164.7m motor finance redress provision driving a =C2=
=A360.3m statutory loss and dividend cancellation at Close Brothers. Other ma=
tters comprised contested High Court claims involving Coutts & Co. and Barcla=
ys, NGO scrutiny of coal financing naming Barclays and HSBC, and Barclays=E2=
=80=99 extension of the transition period for its three-day office attendance=
 mandate following staff opposition.

 Adverse News by Classification Theme

 1. Operational Resilience & Cyber Security

 Santander UK Mobile App & Online Banking Disruption

 01 October

 Customer service disruption occurred on 1 October affecting mobile banking a=
nd login portals. Santander UK acknowledged user reports and confirmed servic=
e restoration at 13:13 BST while conducting root-cause technical reviews.

 Source: GB News (Personal Finance) & Santander UK Telemetry

 Barclays UK Digital Banking Access Disruption

 01 October

 Around 300 user reports concerning digital banking access were recorded on 1=
 October. The report states that the access issues were resolved; it does not=
 specify the outage duration or restoration time.

 Source: Barclays Digital Banking Service Status & Incident Feeds

 2. Regulatory & Enforcement

No qualifying incidents retained under this theme for the reporting week.

 3. Financial Crime & Compliance

 Royal Bank of Scotland Peebles Branch ATM Ram-Raid & Structural Damage

 29 September

 A heavy plant telehandler was used in an early morning ram-raid on the RBS P=
eebles High Street branch, removing two automated teller machines, damaging e=
xternal masonry, and necessitating temporary branch closure. Police Scotland =
initiated a criminal investigation.

 Source: BBC News & Police Scotland Lothian & Borders Division

 4. Prudential & Financial Stability

 Close Brothers FY26 Preliminary Results & Motor Finance Redress Provision

 29 September

 Close Brothers Group plc preliminary FY26 results reported a statutory opera=
ting loss of =C2=A360.3M following an additional =C2=A3164.7M provision for h=
istorical motor finance discretionary commission redress (taking total provis=
ion to ~=C2=A3320M) and cancellation of the final dividend. Group CET1 capita=
l ratio stood at 14.1%.

 Source: London Stock Exchange RNS & Close Brothers Group Investor Relations

 5. Conduct, Legal & Reputational

 Coutts & Co. Defends High Court Lawsuit Concerning Customer Records

 01 October

 Former shipping executive Philip Adkins filed proceedings in the High Court =
King=E2=80=99s Bench Division against Coutts & Company alleging inaccurate in=
ternal customer intelligence records contributed to account closure. Coutts i=
s defending the action and rejects the allegations.

 Source: Financial Times Legal & Courts and Tribunals Judiciary

 Barclays Named in High Court Insolvency Defence by MFS Founder

 28 September

 A defence filed in the High Court by Paresh Raja, founder of Market Financia=
l Solutions (MFS), alleged an account freeze of ~=C2=A3145M in late 2025 impa=
cted group liquidity prior to administration. Barclays firmly rejects the all=
egations, affirming it acted at all times in full accordance with statutory, =
regulatory, and legal obligations.

 Source: The Times Business & High Court Commercial Court

 Barclays and HSBC Named in NGO Coal Financing Study

 30 September

 Environmental research organisations Urgewald and Reclaim Finance published =
a study of European bank financing since COP26. The study named Barclays and =
HSBC in relation to coal financing, according to The Banker. These are NGO fi=
ndings; the supplied report records no regulatory or judicial determination a=
nd does not include a response from either bank.

 Source: The Banker & Urgewald / Reclaim Finance Energy Finance Database

 Barclays delays three-day office mandate after staff backlash

 30 September

 FStech reported on 30 September that Barclays had extended the transition pe=
riod for its planned three-day-a-week UK office attendance requirement to the=
 start of 2027, following opposition backed by thousands of staff. The policy=
 had been due to take effect on 5 October. Staff seeking to defer compliance =
until 2027 require line-manager approval. Unite organised an open letter oppo=
sing the changes, while Barclays said it had listened to employee feedback an=
d was extending the transition to provide support.

 Source: FStech =E2=80=94 Jonathan Easton (30 September 2026)

 Surveillance Perimeter & Exclusions Note (Allegations Cleared / Not Upheld):

Five Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS) consumer complaints indexed during the=
 week concerning Monzo Bank (DRN-6345630), Barclays Bank UK PLC (DRN-6363446)=
, HSBC UK Bank plc (DRN-6370365), Halifax (DRN-6335641), and Santander UK (DR=
N-6506580) were not upheld. These complaints are excluded from the adverse in=
cident count; a complaint not being upheld is not presented as a broader find=
ing that no misconduct occurred.

